# IssueFlow Project Standards

## Technology

- Java version is 17.
- Backend framework is Spring Boot.
- Backend build and dependency management uses Maven only.
- Prefer the Maven Wrapper with `./mvnw`.
- Never introduce Gradle, Gradle Wrapper, or Ant.
- Frontend uses React, TypeScript, and Vite.
- Frontend package management uses Yarn only.
- Never use npm, npx, pnpm, or Bun.
- Never create or commit `package-lock.json`.
- The database is SQLite unless requirements explicitly change.

## Engineering Standards

- Read `ISSUEFLOW_CURSOR_REQUIREMENTS.md` before implementing non-trivial project requirements.
- Prefer the smallest maintainable implementation that satisfies the requirement.
- Fix root causes instead of adding one-off workarounds.
- Do not add unnecessary abstractions, wrappers, dependencies, or architectural layers.
- Do not modify unrelated code.
- Do not silently swallow exceptions.
- Keep business logic explicit and testable.
- Preserve existing behavior unless the requirement explicitly changes it.
- Inspect existing usages before changing a public API, shared type, schema, or interface.

## Constants

- Do not scatter magic values or reusable constant strings through the codebase.
- Backend constants belong under `backend/src/main/java/com/issueflow/constants/`.
- Frontend constants belong under `frontend/src/constants/`.
- Prefer enums for closed domain concepts such as status, severity, priority, and category.
- Organize constants by responsibility rather than creating one oversized constants file.

## Configuration and Secrets

- Never hard-code credentials, secrets, tokens, private keys, or sensitive configuration.
- Any new environment variable must also be added to the appropriate `.env.example`.
- Example secret values must be sanitized.
- Never commit runtime `.env` files.
- Never commit the SQLite runtime database.

## Style Constraints

- Do not use emojis in source code, comments, documentation, UI copy, or generated project text.
- Do not use em dash characters in source code, comments, documentation, UI copy, or generated project text.
- Keep names descriptive and professional.
- Avoid placeholder production-facing text such as `foo`, `bar`, or `lorem ipsum`.

## Before Declaring Work Complete

- Review the generated diff.
- Confirm every changed file is necessary.
- Run relevant tests.
- Run the applicable build or verification command.
- Verify representative normal behavior and important failure paths.
- Confirm the implementation matches the project requirements.
- Report assumptions or known limitations.
